def lesson(slug,title,objective,prerequisites,sections,scenario,tasks,reflection,reference,rubric,sources,chapter):
    spec=unit(slug,title,objective,prerequisites,sections,scenario,
              [question(*task) for task in tasks],reflection,reference,rubric,[])
    spec.update(course_slug=COURSE_SLUG,stage=STAGE,chapter=chapter,estimated_minutes=40,
                sources=[dict(title=SOURCES[key][0],url=SOURCES[key][1]) for key in sources])
    return spec


UNITS=[
lesson('embedding-representations','Embeddings: what a vector represents and what it cannot prove',
 'Explain task-dependent representations, calculate similarity and choose an embedding baseline.',
 'Technical bridge: Python, vectors and retrieval. You can take this course before completing the research path.',[
 ('Start with the task','An embedding maps an input into a fixed-length numeric representation. A text encoder can make related queries and passages close under a chosen similarity measure. Its training objective, examples, pooling and preprocessing shape what closeness means. A sentence representation may pool token representations; it is not simply the token ID list. Models can also represent images, audio or multiple modalities. Equal vector length alone does not make representations from different models compatible: their coordinate systems need not align. Preserve the exact model revision and preprocessing when storing vectors.'),
 ('Work through the geometry','For vectors a and b, cosine similarity is their dot product divided by the product of their Euclidean lengths. With q=[1,0], a=[2,0] and b=[1,1], cosine(q,a)=1 and cosine(q,b)=1/sqrt(2), about .707. Dot products instead are 2 and 1; lengths affect unnormalized dot-product ranking. If vectors are normalized to unit length, dot product equals cosine. Decide explicitly what to do with a zero vector, whose cosine is undefined; do not quietly interpret a numerical fallback as meaningful similarity.'),
 ('Similarity depends on the job','Symmetric similarity asks whether two texts express similar meaning. Asymmetric retrieval asks whether a longer passage answers a short question. Negation, numbers and specialized vocabulary can change the required answer despite broad topical similarity. Follow the chosen model’s documented query/document encodings, prefixes, pooling and maximum input length. There is no prefix rule shared by all models. Inspect truncation: a passage that loses its final exception clause may still look like a strong topic match. Compare with a simple lexical baseline before assuming a dense encoder is better.'),
 ('Separate representation from authority','A close vector can point to an obsolete, unauthorized or factually wrong document. Apply access and version rules before exposing results; inspect whether the retrieved passage actually supports the answer. Cosine .92 is not a calibrated 92% probability of truth or relevance. A threshold needs validation on labeled examples from the intended task, including unanswerable and near-miss cases. Cross-language performance and domain transfer are empirical questions. Record important failure groups, not just an average similarity score.'),
 ('Your first design decision','For a support assistant, preserve a small corpus, document IDs, effective dates, permissions and labeled query-to-passage relevance. Encode a fixed baseline, retrieve candidates and inspect misses. Save model revision, dimension, normalization, tokenizer/preprocessing, similarity rule and corpus hash as an index contract. A model change normally requires re-encoding the document corpus; changing only the query encoder can break compatibility. This lesson establishes the design and arithmetic. A written proposal or toy calculation does not establish performance of a real deployed embedding model.')],
 'A query q=[1,0] is compared with a=[2,0] and b=[1,1]. An engineer calls a cosine score .92 a “92% correctness guarantee” and proposes swapping the query encoder without rebuilding stored passage vectors.',[
 ('What is cosine(q,b), approximately?',['0.500','0.707','1.000','2.000'],1,['That would use an incorrect length.','Correct: 1 divided by sqrt(2).','The vectors do not have the same direction.','That confuses unnormalized scale with cosine.']),
 ('What should happen when changing the embedding model?',['Keep the old passage vectors unchanged.','Treat every new score as a probability.','Copy only the old similarity threshold.','Re-encode passages and evaluate retrieval.'],3,['Different encoders need not share coordinates.','Similarity is not calibrated correctness.','Threshold behavior can change with the model.','Correct: rebuild the compatible representation and test it.'])],
 'Show the similarity calculation, critique the .92 claim and specify the minimum index contract and evaluation for the encoder change.',
 'The cosine is 1/sqrt(2), about .707. A .92 similarity does not establish a truth probability. Preserve model/revision, preprocessing, dimension, normalization, metric and corpus/version/access metadata; re-encode passages with the new encoder and compare labeled retrieval results before choosing thresholds.',
 ['Calculates cosine using both vector lengths','Distinguishes similarity from factual authority','Explains coordinate compatibility and re-encoding','Defines a task-based comparison and index contract'],['embeddings','embedding-training'],0),
lesson('embedding-ingestion-chunking','Build the document pipeline: parsing, chunking and metadata',
 'Design traceable retrieval units that preserve context, permissions and document versions.',
 'Embeddings: representations and similarity.',[
 ('Begin before the encoder','A retrieval pipeline starts with source acquisition, parsing and ownership. Inventory formats, languages, tables, scanned pages and changing documents. Retain source IDs, versions, permissions, timestamps and a way to recover the original passage. OCR and HTML parsing can lose headings, table columns, footnotes or reading order. Inspect representative parsed outputs before measuring the embedding model. A semantically excellent encoder cannot retrieve a clause that parsing discarded. Keep ingestion failures observable rather than silently indexing empty or malformed text.'),
 ('Choose a retrieval unit','A chunk must contain enough evidence to answer a question without overwhelming retrieval with unrelated topics. Compare fixed-token, paragraph/heading-aware and parent-child strategies. Smaller is not automatically more precise: a short fragment may omit the subject or an exception. Larger is not automatically more complete: truncation and mixed topics can hide the relevant span. Respect the tokenizer and input limits of the embedding model. Character counts do not reliably measure token length, especially across languages, code and structured data.'),
 ('Calculate an overlap example','Consider a 1,000-token document, windows of 300 tokens and overlap of 50. With starts at 0,250,500 and 750, the four windows cover 300,300,300 and 250 tokens. You encode 1,150 token positions, including 150 repeated positions. This assumes an explicit final partial window and no extra title prefixes or special tokens. Overlap may preserve a boundary clause, but it also increases storage and creates near-duplicate hits. Deduplicate retrieved evidence or diversify results so five overlapping windows do not masquerade as five independent sources.'),
 ('Keep boundaries meaningful','A refund table might say 30 days in one column and supervisor approval for damaged goods in another. A chunk containing only “30 days” cannot establish the full rule. Preserve headers and qualifiers or retrieve a parent section after finding a child passage. Keep stable relationships between chunk IDs and source spans. Metadata filters should enforce document versions and the requesting user’s access independently of model text. Removing an account’s permission must affect retrieval even when its old vector remains stored; deleting the source should invalidate its chunks and relevant caches.'),
 ('Design the comparison','Fix the source snapshot, questions and relevance labels, then change one chunking choice at a time. Label whether a sufficient evidence span is present, not merely whether a document discusses the topic. Measure retrieval coverage, duplicate crowding, unsupported answers, abstention, latency and index size. Separate parsing loss from chunking loss and ranking loss. Use a development set for choosing settings and an untouched final comparison. Include an exception clause across a boundary and a restricted-document case. A proposed experiment is assessed for its reasoning here; no corpus ingestion job is run by submitting the written answer.')],
 'A 1,000-token policy uses 300-token windows with 50-token overlap and a final partial window. A damaged-goods exception is separated from the ordinary return rule; several overlapping hits all cite the ordinary rule.',[
 ('How many token positions are encoded under that window rule?',['1,000','1,200','1,150','1,500'],2,['Overlap repeats 150 positions.','That assumes four full 300-token windows.','Correct: 300+300+300+250=1,150.','That does not follow the specified starts.']),
 ('Which change best addresses the missing exception?',['Preserve or retrieve its qualifying context.','Increase confidence in the existing answer.','Count overlapping hits as independent proof.','Remove effective dates from the metadata.'],0,['Correct: keep the condition with its applicable rule and test coverage.','Confidence does not supply missing evidence.','Duplicate passages are not independent confirmation.','Version metadata is necessary to select applicable policy.'])],
 'Derive the window count and repeated positions, propose a chunking repair, and define a controlled evaluation that distinguishes parsing, retrieval and answer failures.',
 'Starts 0,250,500,750 give lengths 300,300,300,250: 1,150 encoded positions. Preserve the damaged-goods qualifier with its rule or fetch a parent section; retain source spans, access and effective dates. Keep data/questions fixed and separately check sufficient evidence coverage, duplicate crowding and answer support on new boundary cases.',
 ['Calculates window coverage under the stated convention','Preserves conditions and source provenance','Treats duplicates and access/version filters explicitly','Separates parsing, retrieval and generation measurements'],['embeddings','ir'],0),
lesson('embedding-vector-indexes','Vector search: exact baselines, ANN indexes and hybrid retrieval',
 'Separate encoder quality from index approximation and choose a measurable search configuration.',
 'Document ingestion and chunking.',[
 ('Start with exact search','An exact nearest-neighbor search scores every eligible vector under the declared metric and returns the highest-ranked candidates. It is a useful reference even if a large production corpus eventually needs approximation. Approximate nearest-neighbor search, or ANN, trades resources for speed and may omit neighbors that exact search would return. This is different from the encoder ranking an irrelevant passage highly. Evaluate those failures separately: an index cannot repair an embedding space that puts the wrong documents nearest to the query.'),
 ('Understand the main tradeoffs','HNSW organizes a graph of neighboring vectors; search explores a limited candidate region. Construction choices affect graph quality, while a search-time breadth setting affects the accuracy/latency tradeoff. IVF partitions a vector space and searches selected partitions; probing more partitions can increase coverage and cost. Product quantization compresses representations and introduces approximation error. Exact configuration names, memory behavior and supported filter strategies depend on the engine. Consult its documentation rather than assuming similarly named settings across products are interchangeable.'),
 ('Work the memory and recall example','One million 768-dimensional float32 vectors need 1,000,000×768×4 bytes: 3.072 billion bytes, about 2.86 GiB. That is vector payload alone, excluding graph links, IDs, metadata, caches and replication. For a separate ANN test, exact search returns five reference neighbors for each of 100 queries. If ANN returns 450 of those 500 reference IDs, its neighbor recall@5 is 90%. This does not mean 90% of passages are relevant to the user; the exact reference neighbors themselves may be poor evidence.'),
 ('Use lexical and dense signals deliberately','Dense retrieval can capture semantic relationships, while lexical retrieval can be valuable for exact product codes, names and uncommon terms. Hybrid retrieval combines candidate sets or rankings. A reranker can score query-passage pairs more closely after retrieval, at extra latency and compute cost. Rank-fusion methods avoid treating arbitrary lexical and dense score scales as directly comparable, but still need validation. Keep permission and current-version filtering effective through candidate generation, fusion, reranking and caches. Retrieving a forbidden candidate and exposing it before a final filter is already a problem.'),
 ('Make a search decision','Benchmark exact search first on a fixed compatible vector set. Compare ANN configurations against that neighbor reference and against human relevance labels. Report p50/p95 latency, resource usage, relevant evidence coverage and access failures under realistic filters and load. A query encoder change, index rebuild or quantization change belongs in a versioned release. Preserve the prior configuration and a rollback path. Prefer a measured tradeoff over a fashionable index label. The correct index for a small corpus may simply be exact search; no universal corpus-size threshold determines that choice.')],
 'A million vectors have 768 float32 components. ANN recovers 450 of the 500 exact-search reference neighbors across 100 queries; separate relevance labels have not yet been scored.',[
 ('What does the 90% measurement establish?',['Ninety percent of answers are supported.','Ninety percent of documents are current.','Ninety percent of reference neighbors returned.','Ninety percent of users prefer the system.'],2,['Answer support was not measured.','Current versions were not measured.','Correct: this is ANN neighbor recall against exact search.','No preference study was supplied.']),
 ('What is the approximate vector payload before index overhead?',['0.768 billion bytes.','3.072 billion bytes.','12.288 billion bytes.','768 billion bytes.'],1,['Float32 uses four bytes per component.','Correct: 1,000,000×768×4.','That multiplies by four an extra time.','That miscounts dimensions and bytes.'])],
 'Explain the two different notions of recall, calculate payload memory and propose an exact-versus-ANN and hybrid-search evaluation with access filters.',
 'Payload is 3.072 billion bytes, about 2.86 GiB, before overhead. ANN recovers 90% of exact top-five neighbors; this says nothing yet about human relevance or answer truth. Compare compatible vectors under fixed queries/filters, score labeled evidence and load latency, and evaluate lexical/dense/fused candidates without exposing restricted records.',
 ['Calculates storage with explicit units and exclusions','Separates ANN neighbor recall from relevance','Proposes fixed-data comparisons and latency measurements','Preserves permissions throughout retrieval and caching'],['faiss','ir'],1),
lesson('embedding-evaluation-adaptation','Evaluate and fine-tune an embedding model',
 'Design relevance judgments, contrastive training data and a leakage-resistant embedding comparison.',
 'Embedding geometry, retrieval pipelines and vector indexes.',[
 ('Define what relevant means','Build query-to-document relevance labels around the decision the retriever must support. One passage may answer the question completely, another supply partial context, and a third merely share vocabulary. Save labeling instructions and resolve ambiguous cases. Include exact identifiers, negation, changed policies, multilingual queries and questions with no supporting passage. Separate queries used to develop the system from final evaluation, grouping paraphrases and related documents where needed. Randomly splitting near-duplicate question variants can make generalization appear much easier than it is.'),
 ('Choose metrics for the evidence','Recall@k measures the fraction of labeled relevant items retrieved; precision@k measures the fraction of returned items labeled relevant. Reciprocal rank emphasizes the first relevant result. Graded measures such as nDCG can account for differing relevance levels and position. Define how unjudged documents, empty result lists and unanswerable queries are handled. Retrieval labels are not answer labels: even with good recall, generation may ignore or distort evidence. Keep an answer-support evaluation and the access/version tests separate from ranking quality.'),
 ('Prepare adaptation examples','A contrastive training set can contain query-positive pairs or query-positive-negative groups. Positives must actually answer the query under its context and date. Hard negatives are plausible confusions, not arbitrary unrelated sentences. In-batch-negative objectives often treat other examples’ passages as negatives; another passage may actually be relevant, creating a false negative. Inspect duplicates, alternate valid answers and group structure before choosing a loss and sampler. Preserve dataset permissions and provenance. Match training inputs to the selected encoder’s query/document conventions.'),
 ('Work a comparison','Suppose 20 development queries each have two labeled relevant passages. A baseline retrieves 24 of the 40 relevant items; a candidate retrieves 30. Micro-averaged recall rises from 60% to 75%, or 15 percentage points. The candidate’s recall is 25% higher relative to .60, a different statement. This small development result is not a final test and does not prove downstream answer quality. Inspect paired query-level changes: a gain on easy paraphrases can coexist with regressions on product IDs or authorization-sensitive cases.'),
 ('Decide whether tuning helped','Keep the untuned encoder, a lexical baseline and the adapted encoder under the same corpus, preprocessing, index and query protocol. Choose hyperparameters on development data, then evaluate a held-out set once for the declared comparison. Record model revision, loss, sampling, effective batch, seed and training data snapshot. Rebuild document vectors for an adapted encoder and version the index with it. Check latency, storage, language/domain regressions and unanswerable cases. A lower contrastive training loss is useful debugging evidence, not proof of better retrieval. This unit assesses a concrete experiment design without claiming a real embedding-training run has occurred.')],
 'Twenty queries each need two passages. The baseline finds 24/40 and the candidate 30/40 relevant passages on development data. A proposed in-batch negative is another valid answer to the same query.',[
 ('What is the absolute recall improvement?',['6 percentage points.','15 percentage points.','25 percentage points.','75 percentage points.'],1,['Six is the additional passage count.','Correct: .75−.60=.15.','Twenty-five percent is the relative increase.','Seventy-five percent is the candidate recall itself.']),
 ('How should the alternate valid answer be treated?',['Always relabel it as an irrelevant passage.','Remove every difficult query from evaluation.','Use it as proof that loss never matters.','Avoid treating it as an unquestioned negative.'],3,['That would create a false negative.','Removing hard cases biases the evaluation.','Loss still matters; the labels and sampler need attention.','Correct: inspect relevance and adapt sampling or labels.'])],
 'Calculate absolute and relative gains, diagnose the false-negative risk and specify a held-out comparison and index migration for an adapted encoder.',
 'Recall is .60 versus .75: +15 percentage points or +25% relative. Another valid answer should not be blindly pushed away as a negative. Preserve grouped held-out queries, fixed indexing/access conditions, appropriate labels/loss/sampling and query-level regressions; rebuild compatible document vectors and separately measure answer support.',
 ['Calculates absolute and relative changes correctly','Identifies false negatives and leakage risks','Defines a controlled training and held-out evaluation','Plans compatible re-indexing and downstream checks'],['embedding-training','ir'],1),
lesson('llm-adaptation-strategy','Choose prompting, RAG, continued pretraining or fine-tuning',
 'Match a measured failure to the smallest appropriate adaptation mechanism.',
 'Foundation and technical bridge; understand retrieval and held-out evaluation.',[
 ('Name the failure before the method','A support assistant can fail because it lacks a current document, misunderstands a task, emits invalid structure, lacks domain language or is allowed to perform an unsafe action. These are different mechanisms. Supply current authoritative facts through retrieval when appropriate. Improve instructions and validate structured outputs when the problem is task specification or format. Enforce authorization in application code. Fine-tuning changes weights; it does not itself connect a model to today’s database, create permissions or guarantee factual answers.'),
 ('Separate training stages','Pretraining commonly learns next-token prediction over broad corpora. Continued pretraining applies a language-modeling objective to additional material, often for domain adaptation, and still needs data rights and regression checks. Supervised fine-tuning, or SFT, teaches behavior from demonstrations: given an input, produce a desired response. Preference optimization uses comparisons between responses to shape their relative likelihood or a reward-based objective. These stages can be combined, but changing the training objective changes what evidence is needed to judge success. A stage name is not a quality guarantee.'),
 ('Understand the parameter choice','Full fine-tuning updates the selected base-model weights directly. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning, or PEFT, updates a smaller set of parameters such as adapters while retaining the base. LoRA is one PEFT method, not a synonym for every kind of fine-tuning. A quantized frozen base with trainable adapters changes storage and memory tradeoffs; it does not remove the need for representative examples or held-out tests. Choose a base model using its behavior, license, supported context, runtime constraints and evaluation results, not parameter count alone.'),
 ('Work through a support case','Suppose a policy changed yesterday, an extractor inconsistently follows a stable schema, and a language model misunderstands specialist abbreviations. Retrieve the new policy with effective-date checks. First test the extractor with clear examples and schema enforcement; consider SFT if a stable behavioral gap persists across representative cases. Investigate the abbreviations with a domain evaluation, comparing a glossary/retrieval approach against adaptation. If a refund tool executes without approval, repair the authorization boundary immediately. Training on more successful refunds cannot make an unrestricted tool safe.'),
 ('Write the decision record','For each option, state the observed failure, current baseline, intended behavioral change, data requirements, resource budget, maintenance burden and stop condition. Keep a no-training baseline and avoid combining multiple fixes before measuring them. Fine-tuning can regress capabilities, overfit demonstrations or memorize sensitive data. Retrieval also has failure modes, including missing evidence and poor ranking. Compare the complete systems under a common task protocol. A defensible decision may be to postpone training until better labels or permission controls exist. This course teaches how to design and assess that decision; completing it is not evidence that a real model has been trained.')],
 'The approved policy changed yesterday. Extraction formatting is inconsistent despite adequate source context. A refund executor also lacks an approval check.',[
 ('What should address the missing current policy first?',['Retrieve the approved current source.','Increase sampling randomness for answers.','Memorize one policy in every adapter.','Use training loss as policy authority.'],0,['Correct: provide applicable evidence and test its use.','Randomness does not supply the missing fact.','An adapter is not a reliable mechanism for ongoing policy updates.','Training loss cannot establish the current policy.']),
 ('What enforces refund authorization?',['A longer generic training dataset.','A more fluent generated explanation.','Application permission and approval checks.','A favorable overall language benchmark.'],2,['More data does not enforce a tool boundary.','An explanation is not an authorization check.','Correct: enforce the boundary independently of model output.','An aggregate benchmark does not grant permission.'])],
 'Choose a mechanism for each failure and define a comparison that would justify SFT for the stable behavioral gap.',
 'Use current-source retrieval for policy, test instructions/examples and schema validation before SFT for extraction, and enforce refund approval in application code. Compare the strongest no-training baseline and a tuned candidate on fixed representative held-out cases, including regressions, cost and maintenance; require evidence of a persistent behavior gap.',
 ['Distinguishes facts, behavior and authorization failures','Explains the different training stages and parameter choices','Preserves a competitive no-training baseline','Defines evidence and stop conditions before adaptation'],['sft','peft','cards'],2),
lesson('llm-training-data','Prepare instruction data: provenance, quality and leakage',
 'Specify a trustworthy instruction dataset and an evaluation split that survives duplicates and revisions.',
 'Choose an adaptation mechanism.',[
 ('Treat demonstrations as a specification','An instruction example expresses desired behavior under a particular input and context. Store what the model is allowed to know, the requested output and the approved response. For grounded tasks, a response should be supported by the supplied source version, not by a fact available only to the annotator. Include appropriate abstentions, uncertainty and boundary cases. More rows do not fix inconsistent behavior or incorrect labels. Write an annotation guide, audit a sample and resolve disagreements before treating the dataset as training truth.'),
 ('Preserve permission and provenance','For each data source, record ownership, permitted uses, license or contractual restrictions, consent where applicable and retention/deletion requirements. Public availability is not sufficient evidence of training rights. Remove secrets and unnecessary personal data, including material copied into outputs or metadata. Pseudonymized identifiers may remain identifying when joined to other fields. Synthetic examples require their own provenance and rights review; they can reproduce errors or sensitive fragments from the source system. Dataset cards help communicate these boundaries but do not replace checking them.'),
 ('Split before augmentation','Separate training, validation and final test using the intended deployment boundary: customer, document, task family or time. Group duplicates, near-duplicates and paraphrases together before assigning partitions. Augment training only after the split. If examples from the same support thread appear in training and test, a strong score may reflect recognition rather than transfer. Preserve a final evaluation that is not used to select prompts, data recipes, checkpoints or hyperparameters. Keep the splitting code and hashes so a later dataset refresh can be audited.'),
 ('Work an audit','A proposed dataset contains 1,200 records. An audit identifies 100 duplicate rows, 50 records without training rights and 50 incorrect labels. Assume these three sets are disjoint and all identified rows are removed. Then 1,000 records remain. An 80/10/10 allocation would give 800/100/100 rows only if group and time constraints permit that exact allocation; real grouped partitions may differ. State that assumption explicitly. A claim of 1,200 “clean examples” would ignore both the audit and the information boundary.'),
 ('Inspect token and behavior distributions','Report task/category counts, languages, response lengths, refusal/abstention coverage, rare conditions and missing fields. Token budgets matter: many short rows and a few long rows can contribute very different amounts of loss. Inspect whether templated labels let the model exploit superficial cues. Preserve intended source context within the chosen sequence limit; dropping an exception while keeping its answer can create an impossible demonstration. Version the cleaned records, transformations, split memberships and annotation guide together. Keep a deletion and re-training policy for later rights or quality corrections. The deliverable here is an auditable data plan and worked audit, not a claim that a dataset has passed a real compliance review.')],
 'An audit starts with 1,200 rows and removes 100 duplicates, 50 rights failures and 50 label failures; the sets are disjoint. Paraphrases from the same customer thread are currently spread across partitions.',[
 ('How many rows remain under those assumptions?',['1,050','1,100','1,000','1,200'],2,['That ignores one removal set.','That removes duplicates but not the other failures.','Correct: 1,200−100−50−50=1,000.','That ignores every audit finding.']),
 ('How should related paraphrases be split?',['Assign their group to the same partition.','Place one copy in every evaluation set.','Let the test label choose their partition.','Mix them randomly after augmentation.'],0,['Correct: preserve the intended information boundary.','That creates overlap between training and evaluation.','Test labels should not drive a convenient partition.','That can spread near-duplicates across the boundary.'])],
 'Calculate the cleaned count and conditional split sizes, explain the leakage risk and specify the dataset manifest and quality checks needed before SFT.',
 'There are 1,000 retained rows; 800/100/100 is conditional on feasible group/time constraints. Keep related threads and paraphrases together before augmentation. Record source/rights, transformations, labels, task distributions, token lengths, versions, split membership/hashes, deletion handling and a genuinely held-out final evaluation.',
 ['Uses the disjoint-set assumption correctly','Separates rights and label quality from row count','Prevents duplicate/group/time leakage','Provides an auditable versioned data and evaluation plan'],['data','sft','eval'],2),
lesson('llm-chat-templates-masking','Chat templates, tokenization and loss masking',
 'Inspect the exact token-level supervision instead of treating chat JSON as a training objective.',
 'Instruction data preparation; basic next-token prediction.',[
 ('A conversation is serialized','A message list with roles and text is a data structure. The tokenizer’s chat template turns it into the sequence expected by a particular model, with role boundaries and control tokens. Different models can use different formats. Use the tokenizer and documented template belonging to the selected base revision. Inspect rendered text and token IDs for several examples; do not infer correctness from a JSON preview. Avoid accidentally adding special tokens twice when rendering a template to text and tokenizing it afterward.'),
 ('Choose what receives loss','A causal language-model objective predicts subsequent tokens. For instruction training, you may want loss only on the assistant’s answer rather than on system and user text. Masked label positions are commonly represented by an ignore index such as −100, which is a loss convention, not an actual token to insert into the input. Padding should not become a target. Keep the labels aligned with the trainer/model’s shifting convention; many causal-model implementations shift internally, so manually shifting again can train the wrong correspondence.'),
 ('Work the supervision count','Assume a serialized example contains 40 prompt/control positions, 10 assistant answer positions, and 5 padding positions. Under the stated policy, only those 10 answer positions receive loss. The other 45 labels are ignored. This simplified count assumes the policy has already decided how to treat boundary and end-of-turn tokens; real templates may supervise some of them. Inspect the actual mask instead of guessing from character spans. If padding and end-of-sequence share a token ID, a blanket rule that masks every matching ID can accidentally remove real sequence-ending supervision.'),
 ('Handle truncation and packing','Truncation can remove the answer, the question or a source qualifier. Count non-ignored labels after every transformation and reject examples with no intended target. Packing short examples can reduce padding, but their boundaries still matter. An end token does not by itself guarantee independent attention between packed examples; whether cross-example attention is blocked depends on the packing implementation and mask. Inspect that behavior and decide whether it matches the training objective. Compare token budgets and evaluation on the same serialization conventions.'),
 ('Use trainer options carefully','The referenced TRL SFT documentation distinguishes conversational assistant-only loss from prompt-completion loss. Assistant-only masking depends on a compatible chat template that can identify generation spans. Do not assume a flag can infer assistant spans from an incompatible template. Pin library versions and inspect a decoded minibatch, input IDs, attention mask, labels, number of supervised tokens and final stop behavior before a long run. For an extraction task, check that the intended JSON response and termination are represented correctly. This is a token-inspection exercise and design review; the application does not launch a training job when you submit the explanation.')],
 'A serialized example has 40 prompt/control positions, 10 assistant answer positions and 5 padding positions. Only the answer is supervised. Padding reuses the EOS token ID, which also occurs as a real sequence boundary.',[
 ('How many positions receive loss under the stated policy?',['55','45','40','10'],3,['That would supervise every position, including padding.','That is the number of ignored positions.','Those are prompt/control positions.','Correct: only the 10 answer positions.']),
 ('What is the safer padding-mask approach?',['Mask every occurrence of the EOS ID.','Use actual padding/span masks and inspect labels.','Train on padding to teach longer answers.','Skip token inspection once JSON looks valid.'],1,['That may erase real EOS supervision.','Correct: distinguish padding positions from real boundaries.','Padding is not desired response content.','Valid chat JSON does not establish the token-level objective.'])],
 'Construct the supervision-count explanation, identify the EOS/padding and double-shift risks, and describe checks for a compatible chat template and packed minibatch.',
 'Ten labels are supervised and 45 ignored under the stated boundary policy. Identify padding by its positions/mask rather than erasing all EOS IDs. Follow the framework’s label-shift convention once, verify template generation spans, inspect decoded inputs/labels and attention boundaries, and reject truncations with no valid targets.',
 ['Counts supervised and ignored positions correctly','Explains role serialization and template compatibility','Avoids EOS masking and duplicate label shifting','Checks truncation, packing boundaries and the actual minibatch'],['chat','sft'],3),
lesson('llm-supervised-training','Design a supervised fine-tuning run you can debug',
 'Specify effective batch, optimization, checkpoints and stop conditions for an SFT experiment.',
 'Chat templates, masking and cleaned train/validation/test data.',[
 ('Make the run a reproducible object','Record the base model and tokenizer revisions, dataset/split hashes, code and library versions, template, loss mask, sequence limits, packing policy, seed and trainable parameters. Include precision, device setup, optimizer, learning-rate schedule, effective batch and checkpoint selection rule. A seed alone is not a training protocol. Keep the exact environment and configuration with results, including failed runs. Distinguish a proposed setting from a measured result; a configuration file does not demonstrate that training converged.'),
 ('Understand the loop','Conceptually, render and tokenize a batch, compute the masked loss, backpropagate, accumulate gradients if configured, apply the optimizer and scheduler, then clear gradients at the correct boundary. Validation should avoid optimizer updates and use the declared evaluation mode. Model frameworks handle many details, so confirm their behavior instead of duplicating steps. Gradient clipping may help control large updates, but it cannot repair incorrect labels or a wrongly masked objective. Warmup and decay choices must be documented and selected on development evidence.'),
 ('Calculate effective batch','Assume 1,280 examples per epoch, two devices, a microbatch of two examples per device and gradient accumulation over eight microsteps. The effective example batch is 2×2×8=32, so one full epoch has 40 optimizer updates if the data divides exactly, every example is used once and no partial/drop behavior changes it. This is an example-count calculation. With variable-length sequences and masking, the number of supervised tokens per update can vary greatly. Framework and distributed reduction conventions determine how losses and gradients are normalized.'),
 ('Avoid misleading loss averages','If one microbatch has two supervised tokens and another has 200, averaging their mean losses equally does not reproduce a mean over all 202 tokens. Decide the intended weighting and verify the framework’s accumulation/reduction logic. Track non-ignored token counts, not only row counts. Watch training and validation loss, task accuracy, schema validity, abstention and regression metrics. Falling training loss with worsening validation can indicate overfitting or mismatch; a sudden loss change can also reflect a different mask or data mix. Inspect examples before interpreting the curve.'),
 ('Debug cheaply and stop honestly','Before a long run, inspect a batch, verify nonzero finite gradients on intended parameters and test whether a tiny authorized subset can be learned as a debugging check. Success on that subset is not a final evaluation. Set resource/time limits and save resumable checkpoints with optimizer/scheduler state when needed. Select checkpoints using a predeclared development metric; save the untouched test for the declared final comparison. Restore the baseline if the task gain disappears or safety regressions exceed the gate. The submitted work in this course is a reasoned run plan and calculations; execution and hardware behavior are not certified by the written assessment.')],
 'There are 1,280 examples, two devices, two examples per device per microstep and eight accumulation steps. One microbatch has 2 supervised tokens and the next 200. No examples are dropped and the epoch divides exactly.',[
 ('How many optimizer updates occur per epoch?',['40','80','160','640'],0,['Correct: 1,280/(2×2×8)=40.','That omits one factor of two.','That omits a factor in the effective batch.','That uses only the local microbatch size.']),
 ('What is required for a token-mean objective across those microbatches?',['Equal weight for each mean regardless of size.','A larger report about the training curve.','Normalization reflecting supervised-token counts.','Counting only the number of padding positions.'],2,['That gives the two-token batch disproportionate weight.','Wording does not define the gradient objective.','Correct: verify the framework’s token weighting and reductions.','Padding does not contribute to the intended target loss.'])],
 'Derive effective batch and update count, explain token-weighting risk and define a reproducible checkpoint-selection and regression protocol.',
 'Effective example batch is 32 and the epoch has 40 optimizer steps under the stated assumptions. Token counts vary, so inspect accumulation/reduction normalization rather than equally averaging unequal microbatch means. Preserve the full data/model/template/optimizer protocol, select on validation and require held-out task, format and safety checks before promoting a checkpoint.',
 ['Derives batch and update counts with stated assumptions','Distinguishes example count from supervised-token weighting','Defines the loop, checkpoint and debugging boundaries','Preserves held-out evaluation and explicit regression stop criteria'],['sft','eval'],3),
lesson('llm-lora-adapters','LoRA adapters: rank, target modules and checkpoint compatibility',
 'Derive the low-rank update and specify an adapter that can be reproduced and loaded correctly.',
 'SFT run design and basic matrix dimensions.',[
 ('Write the update explicitly','For a weight matrix W with shape m×n, a basic LoRA update is W plus a scaled BA, where B has shape m×r and A has shape r×n. The adapter uses r(m+n) parameters for that matrix instead of a dense mn-parameter update. In the standard formulation the scale is alpha/r; variants can use other scaling rules. The base weights are commonly frozen while adapters train. Record the implementation and initialization convention; rank, alpha and target-module names alone do not fully identify the experiment.'),
 ('Work the parameter count','For m=n=1000 and r=8, A contains 8,000 entries and B contains 8,000, totaling 16,000. A dense update has 1,000,000 entries, so this matrix’s adapter has 1.6% as many parameters, or 62.5 times fewer entries. Adapting two such matrices gives 32,000 adapter parameters. These are local parameter counts, not a claim that the whole model uses 62.5 times less memory or runs 62.5 times faster. Activations, frozen weights, optimizer state and additional trainable components remain relevant.'),
 ('Choose targets and capacity empirically','Transformer architectures name and arrange projections differently. Inspect the actual model to identify intended attention and/or feed-forward targets; do not copy module names from another architecture blindly. A higher rank supplies a larger update space but also changes optimization, memory and overfitting risk. Compare rank, target coverage, dropout and scaling on development data under a controlled budget. Changing all of them together cannot isolate which choice helped. If you add special tokens or train an output head, specify how those extra trainable parameters are saved and loaded.'),
 ('Separate artifacts from dependencies','An adapter checkpoint generally depends on a specific compatible base model and tokenizer. Save the base identifier and revision, tokenizer/template changes, adapter configuration, trainable-module list and data/training manifest. A small adapter directory is not necessarily a standalone complete model. Test a fresh load in a separate process and compare representative outputs or numerical tolerances with the in-memory checkpoint. Check that the adapter is actually enabled; silently running the base can produce plausible answers and misleading evaluation results.'),
 ('Merge only with a verified plan','Some implementations can merge a low-rank update into compatible base weights for inference. Check support, dtype, quantization and memory requirements rather than assuming a universal merge command. Preserve the unmerged artifacts and compare behavior after merging or re-quantizing. A rollout must keep base, adapter and index dependencies aligned. If a fine-tuned encoder changes embedding space, that also requires a compatible index; a generative adapter change does not by itself justify changing vector dimensions. Choose an adapter configuration because held-out behavior and operational constraints support it, not because its files are small.')],
 'A rank-8 adapter targets two distinct 1000×1000 projection matrices. A teammate ships only the adapter directory and says it is a complete replacement for any base model.',[
 ('How many adapter parameters cover the two matrices?',['16,000','32,000','1,000,000','2,000,000'],1,['That covers only one matrix.','Correct: 2×8×(1000+1000)=32,000.','That is one dense matrix.','That is the count for two dense matrices.']),
 ('Which release artifact is essential for reproducible loading?',['Only the final training-loss screenshot.','Any base with the same parameter count.','Only the size of the adapter directory.','Compatible base/tokenizer revisions and configuration.'],3,['A screenshot cannot reconstruct dependencies.','Parameter count does not establish architectural compatibility.','File size does not specify the base or template.','Correct: preserve and verify the dependency contract.'])],
 'Show the parameter arithmetic, distinguish local parameter savings from total memory and define a fresh-load and merge verification plan.',
 'Each matrix needs 16,000 adapter entries; two need 32,000. The 62.5× reduction applies to this matrix-update parameter count, not total training memory. Preserve base/tokenizer revisions, template, targets, rank/scaling and extra trainable modules; verify adapter-enabled fresh loading and compare behavior before/after any supported merge.',
 ['Derives A/B shapes and parameter counts','Qualifies savings without inventing speed or memory guarantees','Specifies adapter dependencies and target-module choices','Defines reload, enablement and merge checks'],['lora','peft'],4),
lesson('llm-qlora-memory','QLoRA: quantized bases, compute precision and memory budgets',
 'Explain which tensors are quantized, which parameters train and why weight storage is not a GPU-fit guarantee.',
 'LoRA adapters and the SFT training loop.',[
 ('What changes from ordinary LoRA','QLoRA trains low-rank adapters while using a frozen quantized base. The base representation saves storage, while computations use supported numerical formats and kernels; “4-bit weights” does not mean every operation or activation is four-bit. Gradients still pass through computations involving the frozen base to reach trainable adapters. Freezing a weight avoids its parameter update but does not eliminate activation memory. Specify the actual quantization library, hardware support, compute dtype, trainable parameter dtypes and optimizer behavior.'),
 ('Read the paper’s components carefully','The QLoRA paper introduces a four-bit NormalFloat representation, double quantization of quantization constants, and paged optimizers to manage memory spikes. These address particular storage or allocation costs. They do not guarantee unchanged quality on a new task or a fixed GPU memory requirement for every recipe. A modern implementation may expose several quantization choices; record which are enabled. The model card, implementation and hardware documentation determine supported combinations. Distinguish a paper’s reported experiments from a prediction about your own workload.'),
 ('Work a storage lower bound','Assume exactly seven billion base-weight values stored with an idealized four bits per value. Their raw payload is 7,000,000,000×4/8=3.5 billion bytes, about 3.26 GiB. This excludes quantization scales and metadata, unquantized tensors, adapters, gradients, optimizer state, activations, temporary workspaces, caches and allocator overhead. Therefore a four-GiB device is not proven sufficient. Longer sequences and larger microbatches can increase activation requirements substantially even when the base payload is unchanged. Use measured peak allocated and reserved memory for the actual training configuration.'),
 ('Plan a constrained experiment','Start from a compatible documented setup, inspect quantized modules and verify that only intended parameters train. Choose a supported compute precision rather than copying bfloat16 settings onto unsupported hardware. Specify sequence length, microbatch, accumulation, checkpointing and any offload policy. Gradient checkpointing trades additional computation for activation-memory savings. Offload or paging can affect throughput and transfer costs. These mechanisms solve different constraints; reducing one tensor’s size does not imply that all bottlenecks vanish. Keep a smaller or shorter-context debugging run available.'),
 ('Evaluate the resulting artifact','Compare the tuned system with the declared base/no-training baseline and, if relevant, with a non-quantized adapter run under a controlled protocol. Inspect target-task gains, numerical stability, important regressions, latency and deployment compatibility. Save quantization configuration alongside base and adapter dependencies. Fresh-load tests must recreate the intended quantized base and enable the adapter. Do not promise that an adapter can be merged or loaded into every serving engine. This lesson assesses memory arithmetic and an experiment plan. It does not claim Cortex has allocated a GPU, downloaded a base model or executed QLoRA training.')],
 'A seven-billion-weight base uses idealized four-bit storage. A proposed run targets a four-GiB GPU with long sequences; the plan counts only weight payload and assumes every tensor is four-bit.',[
 ('What is the idealized raw base-weight payload?',['3.5 billion bytes.','7.0 billion bytes.','14.0 billion bytes.','28.0 billion bytes.'],0,['Correct: seven billion values times half a byte.','That assumes one byte per value.','That assumes two bytes per value.','That assumes four bytes per value.']),
 ('What can be concluded about the four-GiB device?',['It must fit because adapters are small.','No activation memory is needed at all.','Fit requires a full measured memory budget.','All compute will use four-bit arithmetic.'],2,['That ignores the other memory terms.','Frozen weights do not remove activation requirements.','Correct: payload alone cannot establish peak training fit.','Storage precision and compute precision differ.'])],
 'Calculate the storage bound, identify at least five omitted memory terms and propose a supported precision/checkpointing/measurement plan without claiming hardware fit.',
 'Ideal raw payload is 3.5 billion bytes, about 3.26 GiB. Add scales/metadata, unquantized tensors, adapter weights/gradients, optimizer state, activations, workspaces and allocator overhead. Confirm supported compute precision and kernels, inspect trainable modules, vary sequence/microbatch/checkpointing under a fixed protocol and measure actual peaks and quality before deciding fit.',
 ['Calculates bits-to-bytes with explicit exclusions','Separates frozen-base storage from compute and gradients','Accounts for activation/optimizer/overhead memory','Defines supported runtime, measurement and artifact checks'],['qlora','quant','peft'],4),
lesson('llm-finetuning-evaluation','Evaluate a tuned LLM: task gains, regressions and leakage',
 'Build a paired evaluation that can reject a tuned model despite an improved aggregate score.',
 'SFT/LoRA concepts and a clean held-out dataset.',[
 ('Define the claim and baselines','Specify the task, population, source access, output contract and acceptable errors before looking at results. Compare the tuned model against a competitive base prompt and, where appropriate, a retrieval-assisted baseline. Keep documents, permissions, tools and evaluation instructions aligned so the comparison measures the intended change. If the tuned system has different evidence from the baseline, describe it as a system comparison rather than attributing the difference solely to weight updates. Preserve evaluation versions and keep the test out of training and checkpoint selection.'),
 ('Measure behavior at several boundaries','For extraction, separate parseable schema, correct field values, missing-data behavior and unauthorized actions. For grounded answers, assess evidence retrieval, claim support, correct citation and abstention on missing information. Include important languages, rare categories, long contexts and safety/privacy regressions. Track latency and resource use under the expected serving setup. Token loss or perplexity can diagnose prediction behavior, but neither alone establishes task success or user benefit. A model can imitate labels more closely while becoming less reliable under a specific critical condition.'),
 ('Work a paired result','On a synthetic held-out set of 200 cases, the base succeeds on 160 and the tuned model on 176: 80% versus 88%, an eight-percentage-point increase. Suppose 24 cases improve, eight regress, 152 are correct under both and 16 fail under both; these counts reconcile to 200 and the two totals. Preserve the paired case identities. Averaging two percentages hides the eight regressions. If three regressions expose private data, a launch gate can reject the candidate despite its higher overall task score.'),
 ('Account for uncertainty and judgment','Report denominators and uncertainty appropriate to the sampling unit. If several questions come from the same customer or document, treating every question as independent can overstate precision; resampling or analysis should respect that grouping. Use blinded human review for ambiguous quality judgments where feasible, with a rubric and disagreement process. A model judge can be useful but can share biases with the generator. Test judges with supported, incorrect, empty and manipulative controls, and do not let a stylistic preference override an objective answer or access failure.'),
 ('Make a bounded release decision','Write acceptance thresholds and non-negotiable failure gates before choosing the final checkpoint. Inspect error categories and training/test overlap instead of tuning repeatedly on the same final cases. If a regression is repaired after seeing the test, use fresh evaluation evidence for a new claim. Keep the base available for rollback and document limitations that remain. The example numbers are supplied teaching data, not results measured from Cortex learners or a trained model. The course assessment checks your interpretation and release reasoning; it does not convert a written evaluation plan into an executed benchmark.')],
 'Of 200 held-out cases, the base passes 160 and the tuned model 176. There are 24 improvements and eight regressions. Three regressions reveal information outside the requester’s permissions.',[
 ('What is the absolute task-score gain?',['4 percentage points.','8 percentage points.','10 percentage points.','16 percentage points.'],1,['The scores differ by eight points.','Correct: 88% minus 80%.','Ten percent is the relative improvement over .80.','Sixteen is the additional correct-case count.']),
 ('What is the defensible launch decision?',['Launch solely because the average increased.','Delete the three privacy cases from the report.','Treat training loss as the only safety check.','Hold release and repair the access regressions.'],3,['Critical failures need their own gates.','Removing difficult cases hides the regression.','Training loss does not enforce permissions.','Correct: improve safety and gather fresh evidence before promotion.'])],
 'Reconcile the paired counts, calculate absolute/relative gains and write an evaluation and release decision that handles critical regressions and uncertain judgments.',
 'Both-correct=152, both-wrong=16; wins=24 and losses=8 reconcile to 200. Accuracy rises .80 to .88: +8 points or +10% relative. Preserve case/group identities, evaluate support/format/abstention and judge reliability separately, and block release on the privacy failures pending repair and fresh evidence.',
 ['Reconciles paired outcomes and percentage units','Separates aggregate performance from critical failures','Addresses leakage, grouped uncertainty and judge limits','Sets explicit release and fresh-evidence requirements'],['eval','cards'],5),
lesson('llm-preference-optimization','Preference learning: DPO, reward models and reliable labels',
 'Explain what preference pairs teach and distinguish DPO from reward-model-based optimization.',
 'Supervised fine-tuning, likelihood and held-out evaluation.',[
 ('A preference label is a decision rule','A preference example usually contains a prompt/context and responses labeled preferred and dispreferred under a specified criterion. The label may reflect correctness, helpfulness, safety, style or a mixture; these are not interchangeable. State the rubric, source evidence and tie policy. If annotators prefer confident unsupported answers, optimization can reinforce that behavior. Preserve input context, data rights and annotator disagreement. A refusal can be appropriate for one request and unhelpful for another; labeling every refusal as preferred creates the wrong task.'),
 ('Separate optimization families','A reward-model-based pipeline can fit a model to preference data and use that reward signal during policy optimization. It has additional modeling and optimization failure modes, including exploiting an imperfect reward. Direct Preference Optimization, or DPO, uses a preference objective involving response probabilities relative to a reference policy; it does not require training a separate explicit reward model as a prerequisite. This does not mean it needs no comparison distribution or no evaluation. Preserve the intended reference model and the exact response/token likelihood convention.'),
 ('Work a relative-likelihood margin','For one simplified DPO pair, let the preferred response’s policy-versus-reference log-probability difference be .4 and the dispreferred response’s difference be −.2. Their contrast is .6. With beta=.1, the scaled margin is .06. The logistic preference term is sigmoid(.06), about .515, and the negative log of that term is about .664. This is an objective calculation, not a 51.5% probability that the answer is true. Beta scales this objective’s margin; it is not the sampling temperature used when generating an answer.'),
 ('Inspect data and implementation','Use chosen/rejected responses to the same actual prompt and evidence. Check truncation, chat formatting, masking and whether likelihoods are summed or otherwise normalized under the selected loss variant. Length and stylistic cues can dominate labels. If using adapters or precomputed reference scores, verify that the intended reference distribution is actually reproduced; silently changing or disabling the wrong adapter can change the objective. Choose hyperparameters and variants on development data, and keep preference pairs or near-duplicate prompts from leaking into final evaluation.'),
 ('Require evidence beyond preference loss','Compare task correctness, grounding, refusals, privacy, language groups and important general capabilities before and after optimization. Use objective checks where possible and a blinded rubric for subjective aspects. Include adversarial cases that expose superficial reward strategies. Lower preference loss can reflect better agreement with a flawed label distribution rather than better user outcomes. Revisit the labeling rubric if it rewards false confidence or penalizes justified uncertainty. The submitted exercise is a preference-data and evaluation design, not an executed DPO or reinforcement-learning run. A real deployment still needs the separate validation and release controls from the evaluation lesson.')],
 'A preferred response has policy/reference log-probability difference .4 and a rejected response −.2. Beta is .1. Annotators currently prefer confident wording even when the supplied evidence is missing.',[
 ('What is the scaled relative margin?',['0.600','0.020','0.060','0.400'],2,['That omits beta.','That does not use the preferred-minus-rejected contrast.','Correct: .1×(.4−(−.2))=.06.','That is only the preferred response’s difference.']),
 ('What should be repaired before optimizing these labels?',['The rubric rewarding unsupported confidence.','The test set so it contains easier prompts.','The report so refusals are always called failures.','The labels so every long answer is preferred.'],0,['Correct: define support, uncertainty and task-specific quality.','An easier test hides the failure.','Appropriate refusals and abstentions depend on the case.','Length is not evidence of correctness.'])],
 'Explain the margin calculation, the reference-policy role and a preference collection/evaluation protocol that avoids rewarding false confidence.',
 'The contrast is .6 and beta scales it to .06; this objective term is not truth probability or sampling temperature. Preserve the intended reference distribution and masking/likelihood convention. Label supported answers, appropriate abstentions and violations under a clear rubric, record disagreements and evaluate objective correctness and critical regressions on separate cases.',
 ['Calculates the reference-relative margin correctly','Distinguishes DPO from explicit reward-model pipelines','Diagnoses flawed preference labels and reference setup','Defines independent task and regression evaluation'],['dpo','dpo-paper'],5),
lesson('llm-distillation','Distillation: transfer useful behavior without copying mistakes',
 'Design teacher/student data and comparisons without assuming generated answers are ground truth.',
 'Instruction data quality, SFT and paired evaluation.',[
 ('Specify what is transferred','Distillation trains a student to approximate useful behavior from a teacher or ensemble. In classical soft-target distillation, a student can learn from a softened teacher distribution as well as task labels. Temperature changes how the distribution reveals relative preferences among alternatives. In response-based LLM distillation, generated demonstrations can instead become supervised data. A teacher’s internal logits may not be available, and response imitation is not the same experiment as training on its full token distribution. State which signals your method actually uses.'),
 ('Define the goal and rights','The student may target lower latency, a constrained deployment environment, lower resource use or better behavior on a narrow workflow. Check the teacher service’s terms, output-use permissions, base-model license and source-data rights before generating a dataset. Generated content can contain personal data, unsupported claims and copyrighted source material. Keep provenance, generation settings and filtering decisions. A smaller parameter count is not proof of lower end-to-end latency or lower total cost; compare the actual serving and review workload.'),
 ('Build and audit demonstrations','Generate on training prompts that represent the intended workflow, with the correct allowed source context. Check answers against objective labels, validators or qualified human review instead of accepting teacher confidence. Preserve uncertainty and appropriate abstention. Avoid selecting only easy cases that make the teacher look good. If filtering removes difficult groups, record the resulting coverage gap. Do not generate training demonstrations from the final evaluation prompts and then claim the student generalized to those prompts. Synthetic data can reproduce the teacher’s systematic biases or amplify them through repeated reuse.'),
 ('Work a budget example','Assume 10,000 generated demonstrations require 1,500 input and 300 output tokens each. Generation totals 15 million input tokens and three million output tokens before retries, filtering or review. These are workload counts, not a price quote or subscription-availability promise. If an audit rejects 20% of examples, 8,000 remain, but discarded generations still consumed resources. A filtering pass does not establish that every remaining example is correct. Record validation methods and sampled error rates with their denominators.'),
 ('Compare systems fairly','Evaluate the student against the teacher and the untuned student on the same held-out inputs and authorized evidence. Separate teacher imitation from task correctness: agreement with a mistaken teacher is still a task failure. Report latency distributions, throughput, resource use, answer support, refusals and important group regressions. State any weaker capabilities accepted for the deployment constraint and keep a fallback path. If the student uses shorter prompts, a different retriever or a changed output limit, qualify the comparison accordingly. This exercise asks for a distillation plan and resource arithmetic; it does not claim a teacher dataset or student checkpoint has been produced.')],
 'A teacher generates 10,000 examples, each using 1,500 input and 300 output tokens. An audit rejects 20%. A student repeats a teacher error on an untouched evaluation question.',[
 ('What workload and retained count follow from the assumptions?',['15m input, 3m output; 8,000 retained.','12m input, 2.4m output; 8,000 retained.','15m input, 3m output; 10,000 retained.','3m input, 15m output; 2,000 retained.'],0,['Correct: rejected outputs still consumed generation resources.','That subtracts already-consumed generation work.','Twenty percent were rejected.','That swaps token directions and retained/rejected counts.']),
 ('How should the shared teacher/student error be counted?',['A task success because the two models agree.','A task failure despite imitation agreement.','An invalid test because the teacher was wrong.','A reason to add that exact test to training now.'],1,['Agreement does not establish the answer is correct.','Correct: measure the task independently of teacher imitation.','The error is evidence about the systems.','That contaminates the evaluation boundary.'])],
 'Calculate generation work and retained examples, then specify rights, filtering, held-out comparisons and failure handling for a narrow student model.',
 'Generation used 15m input and 3m output tokens; 8,000 examples remain after rejection. Preserve rights/provenance, audit teacher outputs against real task criteria, protect the test set and compare teacher/base-student/tuned-student under fixed evidence and runtime conditions. A shared wrong answer is a task failure even when imitation succeeds.',
 ['Calculates generation resources separately from accepted data','Addresses teacher/output/data permissions and quality','Keeps imitation distinct from task correctness','Defines leakage-resistant runtime and regression comparisons'],['distill','sft','data'],6),
lesson('llm-adapter-deployment','Deploy, monitor and roll back a tuned model',
 'Specify a versioned release that binds data, base, adapter, tokenizer and retrieval dependencies.',
 'Adapters, quantization and held-out evaluation.',[
 ('Define the complete release','A tuned model release is more than an adapter file. Record base and tokenizer revisions, template, adapter configuration and weights, quantization/compute settings, serving code and relevant retrieval/index versions. Preserve data and evaluation manifests, approved usage boundaries and known limitations. Verify licenses and data permissions for the intended distribution and deployment. An internal course completion record is not evidence that these operational checks have been executed for a real model; the assessed deliverable is a concrete, inspectable release plan.'),
 ('Test a fresh process','Load the declared base and tokenizer, enable the intended adapter and reproduce the output serialization and stop behavior in a fresh serving process. Check that required special tokens and any trained extra modules are present. Compare representative outputs or tolerances against the evaluated checkpoint. If merging, converting or re-quantizing, treat the resulting artifact as a changed candidate and check parity and regressions. A serving engine may support inference for a model while not supporting a particular adapter, precision or merge procedure. Confirm that compatibility explicitly.'),
 ('Set admission and release gates','Measure context limits, latency, throughput and memory under realistic concurrency and input lengths. Define timeouts, backpressure and failure responses. Canary or shadow comparisons need observable decisions and a rollback owner. Keep privacy/authorization controls in application code even if the model usually follows them. Preserve a way to restore the previous compatible stack; rolling back only an adapter while leaving an incompatible tokenizer or embedding index can create a new failure. Protect customer data in monitoring and retain only what is necessary for the declared purpose.'),
 ('Work a canary decision','Suppose the predeclared canary gate requires schema failures no higher than 2% and zero unauthorized disclosures. In 1,000 cases, 30 fail schema validation and one exposes unauthorized information. The schema-failure rate is 3%, and the disclosure gate also fails. A higher overall helpfulness score does not satisfy either gate. Stop or roll back under the agreed plan, investigate the affected cases, repair the cause and evaluate a new candidate. These supplied counts illustrate a decision rule; they do not establish production rates for Cortex or any real model.'),
 ('Monitor change instead of assuming permanence','Observe task errors, abstention, schema validity, access violations, latency, cost/resource pressure and relevant group coverage. Separate data or policy changes from model drift and infrastructure failures. A new policy may require retrieval/index maintenance rather than another training run. Capture reviewed failure examples into a versioned development dataset without leaking them into the preserved final test. Changes to prompts, adapters, retrieval, quantization and serving each need an appropriate comparison. Assign ownership for source/data refresh, incidents and re-evaluation. Release only what the evidence supports, and describe the conditions under which the model should not be used.')],
 'A 1,000-case canary has 30 schema failures and one unauthorized disclosure. The gate allows at most 2% schema failures and no disclosures. The team proposes rolling back only the adapter despite a tokenizer change.',[
 ('What is the schema-failure rate and gate result?',['0.3%; both gates pass.','2.0%; only schema passes.','3.0%; both gates fail.','30.0%; only privacy passes.'],2,['Thirty out of 1,000 is three percent, and a disclosure occurred.','The observed schema rate exceeds two percent.','Correct: schema is 3%, and zero-disclosure policy also fails.','That miscalculates the rate and ignores the disclosure.']),
 ('What must the rollback restore?',['Only the model’s display name.','Only the latest adapter file.','Only the successful test cases.','The previous compatible release dependencies.'],3,['A label does not restore behavior.','The tokenizer change can make that combination incompatible.','Removing failures is not a rollback.','Correct: restore the validated base/tokenizer/adapter and other coupled components.'])],
 'Calculate the canary outcome and write a release manifest, fresh-load check, monitoring plan and dependency-aware rollback procedure.',
 'Schema failures are 30/1000=3%, exceeding the 2% gate; one disclosure also fails the zero-disclosure requirement. Hold or roll back the compatible stack, including the tokenizer and adapter dependencies, inspect failures and require new evaluation before promotion. Preserve manifests, runtime limits, privacy-aware monitoring and clear incident/rollback ownership.',
 ['Calculates the canary rates and applies both gates','Binds base, adapter, tokenizer and serving dependencies','Verifies fresh loading and converted/quantized artifacts','Defines privacy-aware monitoring, ownership and rollback'],['cards','peft','quant'],6),
]
